        {
          "name": "From an equation to a transfer function",
          "tags": [],
          "metadata": {
            "description": "<p>Laplace transform of a derivative with zero initial conditions</p>",
            "licence": "None specified"
          },
          "statement": "<p>A system obeys \\( \\dot y + 2y = u \\), starting from rest. What is its transfer function \\( Y(s)/U(s) \\)?</p>",
          "advice": "<ol><li>Transform every term, using \\( \\dot y \\to sY \\) because the system starts from rest. That gives \\( sY + 2Y = U \\).</li><li>Collect \\( Y \\): \\( (s+2)Y = U \\).</li><li>Divide: \\( Y/U = \\dfrac{1}{s+2} \\).</li></ol><p>The denominator, \\( s+2 \\), is the characteristic polynomial, and its root \\( s = -2 \\) is the pole. This is the move behind every block diagram in the unit, so it is worth being quick at.</p>",
          "rulesets": {},
          "extensions": [],
          "builtin_constants": {},
          "constants": [],
          "variables": {},
          "variablesTest": {
            "condition": "",
            "maxRuns": 100
          },
          "ungrouped_variables": [],
          "variable_groups": [],
          "functions": {},
          "preamble": {
            "js": "",
            "css": ""
          },
          "parts": [
            {
              "type": "1_n_2",
              "marks": 1,
              "showCorrectAnswer": true,
              "showFeedbackIcon": true,
              "minMarks": 0,
              "maxMarks": 0,
              "shuffleChoices": false,
              "displayType": "radiogroup",
              "displayColumns": 0,
              "showCellAnswerState": true,
              "choices": [
                "<p>\\( \\dfrac{1}{s+2} \\)</p>",
                "<p>\\( \\dfrac{s}{s+2} \\)</p>",
                "<p>\\( \\dfrac{2}{s+1} \\)</p>",
                "<p>\\( s + 2 \\)</p>"
              ],
              "matrix": [
                "1",
                "0",
                "0",
                "0"
              ],
              "distractors": [
                "<p>Right. Every term transforms, the \\( s \\) stays with \\( Y \\) on the left, and what's left on top is the coefficient of \\( u \\).</p>",
                "<p>The \\( s \\) from \\( \\dot y \\) has ended up in the numerator. It belongs with \\( y \\): the left-hand side collects to \\( (s+2)Y \\), so the numerator is whatever multiplies \\( u \\), which here is 1.</p>",
                "<p>The two coefficients have swapped. The coefficient of \\( y \\), which is 2, joins \\( s \\) in the denominator; the coefficient of \\( u \\), which is 1, is the numerator.</p>",
                "<p>That's the characteristic polynomial, or equivalently \\( U/Y \\) — the right answer upside down. A transfer function is always output over input.</p>"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "Poles and what they mean",
          "tags": [],
          "metadata": {
            "description": "<p>reading stability and oscillation from pole locations</p>",
            "licence": "None specified"
          },
          "statement": "<p>\\( G(s) = \\dfrac{5}{s^2 + 2s + 5} \\) has poles at \\( -1 \\pm 2j \\). Its step response is:</p>",
          "advice": "<p>Read a complex pair \\( \\sigma \\pm j\\omega_d \\) as two separate facts:</p><ol><li><strong>the real part \\( \\sigma \\) sets the envelope.</strong> Negative decays like \\( e^{\\sigma t} \\), positive grows, zero neither.</li><li><strong>the imaginary part \\( \\omega_d \\) sets the ringing.</strong> Non-zero means it oscillates at \\( \\omega_d \\) rad/s; zero means it doesn't.</li></ol><p>Here \\( \\sigma = -1 \\) and \\( \\omega_d = 2 \\), so the response rings at 2 rad/s inside an \\( e^{-t} \\) envelope — most of it gone within about four seconds.</p>",
          "rulesets": {},
          "extensions": [],
          "builtin_constants": {},
          "constants": [],
          "variables": {},
          "variablesTest": {
            "condition": "",
            "maxRuns": 100
          },
          "ungrouped_variables": [],
          "variable_groups": [],
          "functions": {},
          "preamble": {
            "js": "",
            "css": ""
          },
          "parts": [
            {
              "type": "1_n_2",
              "marks": 1,
              "showCorrectAnswer": true,
              "showFeedbackIcon": true,
              "minMarks": 0,
              "maxMarks": 0,
              "shuffleChoices": false,
              "displayType": "radiogroup",
              "displayColumns": 0,
              "showCellAnswerState": true,
              "choices": [
                "<p>unstable</p>",
                "<p>stable, and settles without oscillating</p>",
                "<p>stable, with a decaying oscillation</p>",
                "<p>an oscillation that never decays</p>"
              ],
              "matrix": [
                "0",
                "0",
                "1",
                "0"
              ],
              "distractors": [
                "<p>Stability is decided by the <em>real</em> part, and here it is \\( -1 \\). A complex pole isn't unstable in itself — it's what makes a response oscillate rather than what makes it grow.</p>",
                "<p>The stability half is right, but the \\( \\pm 2j \\) has been dropped. A non-zero imaginary part means the response oscillates on its way in, here at 2 rad/s.</p>",
                "<p>Right — and you read both halves. Real part negative, so it decays; imaginary part non-zero, so it oscillates while decaying.</p>",
                "<p>That needs the poles <em>on</em> the imaginary axis, at \\( \\pm 2j \\) with no real part at all. The \\( -1 \\) is what makes it die away.</p>"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "Natural frequency and damping ratio",
          "tags": [],
          "metadata": {
            "description": "<p>the standard second-order form</p>",
            "licence": "None specified"
          },
          "statement": "<p>For \\( G(s) = \\dfrac{25}{s^2 + 4s + 25} \\), give the natural frequency \\( \\omega_n \\), in rad/s, and the damping ratio \\( \\zeta \\).</p>",
          "advice": "<p>Match the denominator against the standard form \\( s^2 + 2\\zeta\\omega_n s + \\omega_n^2 \\), one coefficient at a time:</p><ol><li><strong>constant term:</strong> \\( \\omega_n^2 = 25 \\), so \\( \\omega_n = 5 \\) rad/s.</li><li><strong>middle term:</strong> \\( 2\\zeta\\omega_n = 4 \\), and \\( \\omega_n \\) is now known, so \\( \\zeta = 4/(2 \\times 5) = 0.4 \\).</li></ol><p>Always do them in that order — the middle term needs \\( \\omega_n \\), so there is nothing to be gained by starting there. A quick check: \\( \\zeta \\) between 0 and 1 means an oscillatory response, which matches the complex poles this denominator has.</p><p>If you got one of these:</p><ul><li><strong>\\( \\omega_n = 25 \\)</strong> — that's \\( \\omega_n^2 \\). The constant term is the square, so take the root.</li><li><strong>\\( \\zeta = 0.16 \\)</strong> — you divided 4 by \\( \\omega_n^2 \\). The middle coefficient is \\( 2\\zeta\\omega_n \\), so divide by \\( 2\\omega_n = 10 \\).</li><li><strong>\\( \\zeta = 0.8 \\)</strong> — you divided by \\( \\omega_n \\) but not by the 2. It is easy to lose; the factor of 2 is there so that \\( \\zeta = 1 \\) is exactly critical damping.</li><li><strong>\\( \\zeta = 2 \\)</strong> — that's \\( \\zeta\\omega_n \\), which is the decay rate \\( \\sigma \\) — a useful number, but not the damping ratio.</li></ul>",
          "rulesets": {},
          "extensions": [],
          "builtin_constants": {},
          "constants": [],
          "variables": {},
          "variablesTest": {
            "condition": "",
            "maxRuns": 100
          },
          "ungrouped_variables": [],
          "variable_groups": [],
          "functions": {},
          "preamble": {
