        {
          "name": "Overshoot",
          "tags": [],
          "metadata": {
            "description": "<p>the link between damping and overshoot</p>",
            "licence": "None specified"
          },
          "statement": "<p>A second-order system with \\( \\zeta = 0.4 \\) is given a step. Its percentage overshoot is closest to:</p>",
          "advice": "<p>Exactly, \\( M_p = e^{-\\pi\\zeta/\\sqrt{1-\\zeta^2}} \\), which for \\( \\zeta = 0.4 \\) gives 0.254, so about 25%.</p><p>In practice nobody evaluates that in a design meeting. Carry three anchors instead and interpolate:</p><ol><li>\\( \\zeta = 0.2 \\) → about 50%</li><li>\\( \\zeta = 0.4 \\) → about 25%</li><li>\\( \\zeta = 0.7 \\) → about 5%</li></ol><p>Less damping, more overshoot, and the curve is steep — which is why phase margin, its frequency-domain cousin, is worth watching closely in week 4.</p>",
          "rulesets": {},
          "extensions": [],
          "builtin_constants": {},
          "constants": [],
          "variables": {},
          "variablesTest": {
            "condition": "",
            "maxRuns": 100
          },
          "ungrouped_variables": [],
          "variable_groups": [],
          "functions": {},
          "preamble": {
            "js": "",
            "css": ""
          },
          "parts": [
            {
              "type": "1_n_2",
              "marks": 1,
              "showCorrectAnswer": true,
              "showFeedbackIcon": true,
              "minMarks": 0,
              "maxMarks": 0,
              "shuffleChoices": false,
              "displayType": "radiogroup",
              "displayColumns": 0,
              "showCellAnswerState": true,
              "choices": [
                "<p>5%</p>",
                "<p>10%</p>",
                "<p>25%</p>",
                "<p>50%</p>"
              ],
              "matrix": [
                "0",
                "0",
                "1",
                "0"
              ],
              "distractors": [
                "<p>That's roughly \\( \\zeta = 0.7 \\) — the value often quoted as a good compromise, so it is an easy one to reach for. This system is less damped than that.</p>",
                "<p>That's roughly \\( \\zeta = 0.6 \\). You're in the right region but a little too damped; the relationship is steep here, so small changes in \\( \\zeta \\) move the overshoot a lot.</p>",
                "<p>Right. \\( M_p = 25.4\\% \\), and the useful thing to carry away is the pairing: \\( \\zeta = 0.4 \\) with about a quarter overshoot.</p>",
                "<p>That's roughly \\( \\zeta = 0.2 \\), considerably livelier than this. Half the height of the step as overshoot would be a very underdamped system.</p>"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "Steady state",
          "tags": [],
          "metadata": {
            "description": "<p>the final value theorem, or DC gain</p>",
            "licence": "None specified"
          },
          "statement": "<p>What does the step response of \\( G(s) = \\dfrac{3}{s+2} \\) settle to?</p>",
          "advice": "<p>The steady-state value of a step response is just \\( G(0) \\), the DC gain. Set \\( s = 0 \\): \\( G(0) = 3/2 = 1.5 \\).</p><p>If you would rather use the final value theorem, the step is \\( 1/s \\), so</p><p>$$ \\lim_{s\\to0} s \\cdot G(s) \\cdot \\frac{1}{s} = \\lim_{s\\to0} G(s) = G(0). $$</p><p>The \\( s \\) and the \\( 1/s \\) cancel, which is why the shortcut works. The theorem is only valid when the response actually settles, so check the poles are in the left half plane first — here the single pole is at \\( s = -2 \\), so it does.</p><p>If you got one of these:</p><ul><li><strong>3</strong> — you took the numerator alone. Setting \\( s = 0 \\) leaves the denominator's constant term too, so it's \\( 3/2 \\), not 3.</li><li><strong>0</strong> — you may have applied \\( \\lim_{s \\to 0} sG(s) \\), which is the final value of the <em>impulse</em> response. For a step the \\( s \\) in the theorem cancels the \\( 1/s \\) of the step itself.</li><li><strong>0.667</strong> — that's \\( 2/3 \\) — the right two numbers, inverted.</li></ul>",
          "rulesets": {},
          "extensions": [],
          "builtin_constants": {},
          "constants": [],
          "variables": {},
          "variablesTest": {
            "condition": "",
            "maxRuns": 100
          },
          "ungrouped_variables": [],
          "variable_groups": [],
          "functions": {},
          "preamble": {
            "js": "",
