              ],
              "distractors": [
                "<p>It does do this — arguably the main reason feedback exists. A disturbance shows up in the measurement, the error changes, and the controller pushes back.</p>",
                "<p>It does do this. It is why a feedback design survives an aircraft getting lighter as it burns fuel, and it is what the sensitivity function measures.</p>",
                "<p>It does do this, and you will see it in the first session: the Quanser rig cannot be flown open-loop by hand, and closing the loop is what makes it flyable.</p>",
                "<p>Right, and this is the one that catches people. Feedback acts on what the sensor <em>says</em>. It cannot tell noise from real motion, so it faithfully corrects for both.</p>"
              ]
            }
          ]
